<!--
  Local single-target build override (sample).

  Building the multi-targeted Uno projects normally restores/evaluates EVERY target
  framework (android, ios, wasm, desktop, windows) even when you pass `-f <one-tfm>` —
  so a plain build of the desktop or Windows head still demands the android/ios/wasm
  workloads. To build just one platform (and need only that platform's workload):

    1. Copy this file to `crosstargeting-override.props` (same folder). That name is
       gitignored, so it only affects YOUR machine — CI and other devs are untouched.
    2. Set <SingleTargetFramework> below to the one TFM you want.
    3. Build / open the solution as usual. Delete the copy to restore full multi-target.

  Equivalent one-off on the command line (no file needed):
    dotnet build src/GcToolkit/GcToolkit.csproj -p:SingleTargetFramework=net10.0-desktop

  The property is opt-in: only the cross-targeted Uno projects read it, so any non-Uno
  single-target project you add later (e.g. a plain net10.0 library) is unaffected.

  Valid values: net10.0-desktop | net10.0-windows10.0.26100 | net10.0-browserwasm
                net10.0-android  | net10.0-ios
-->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <SingleTargetFramework>net10.0-windows10.0.26100</SingleTargetFramework>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
